---INSTRUCTION---
Write a function named `largest_perfect_square` that takes a list of integers and returns the largest integer in the list that is a perfect square. If the list contains no perfect squares, return `nil`. A perfect square is a non-negative integer that can be expressed as the square of an integer. The function should handle empty lists and lists with negative numbers appropriately. The function should run in linear time with respect to the length of the input list.
---TEST---
defmodule SolutionTest do
  use ExUnit.Case, async: false

  test "returns 49 for list [16, 25, 36, 49, 50]" do
    assert Solution.largest_perfect_square([16, 25, 36, 49, 50]) == 49
  end

  test "returns 121 for list [25, 5, 100, 2000, 121]" do
    assert Solution.largest_perfect_square([25, 5, 100, 2000, 121]) == 121
  end

  test "returns 1 for list [1, 2, 3]" do
    assert Solution.largest_perfect_square([1, 2, 3]) == 1
  end
end
---REFERENCE---
defmodule Solution do
  @doc """
  Returns the largest perfect square in a list of integers, or nil if none.
  """
  def largest_perfect_square(lst) do
    squares = Enum.filter(lst, &perfect_square?/1)
    case squares do
      [] -> nil
      _ -> Enum.max(squares)
    end
  end

  defp perfect_square?(n) when n >= 0 do
    root = round(:math.sqrt(n))
    root * root == n
  end

  defp perfect_square?(_), do: false
end
